/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Odd Even Linked List
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Linked List
Difficulty : Medium

Problem:
Given the head of a singly linked list, group all the nodes with 
odd indices together followed by the nodes with even indices, and 
return the reordered list. The first node is considered odd, and 
the second node is even, and so on. The relative order inside 
both groups must remain as it was in the input.

Approach:
We can solve this problem in-place by maintaining two separate 
pointers: one for the odd-indexed nodes and one for the even-indexed 
nodes. As we traverse the linked list, we rearrange the `next` 
pointers to link all odd nodes together and all even nodes together. 
Finally, we connect the end of the odd list to the head of the even list.

Why does this work?
Because we only need to rearrange the existing nodes without creating 
new ones, we can achieve this in O(1) extra space. By keeping track 
of the starting node of the even list (e.g., `evenHead`), we can 
safely detach and reattach nodes. The `odd` pointer always jumps to 
the next odd node (`even->next`), and the `even` pointer always jumps 
to the next even node (`odd->next`). This ensures that the relative 
order is preserved and no nodes are lost during traversal.

Algorithm:
1. Handle edge cases: If the list is empty or has only one node, 
   return `head` directly.
2. Initialize an `odd` pointer to `head` (the first node).
3. Initialize an `even` pointer to `head->next` (the second node).
4. Store the starting node of the even list in a separate pointer 
   `evenHead` to connect it later.
5. Traverse the list using a `while` loop that continues as long 
   as `even` and `even->next` are not null.
6. Inside the loop, link the current `odd` node to the next odd 
   node (`even->next`), and advance the `odd` pointer.
7. Link the current `even` node to the next even node (`odd->next`), 
   and advance the `even` pointer.
8. After the loop terminates, link the last `odd` node to `evenHead`.
9. Return the modified `head`.

Time Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
We traverse the linked list exactly once, updating pointers in 
constant time for each node, where n is the number of nodes in 
the linked list.

Space Complexity : O(1)

Reason:
We only use a few extra pointers (`odd`, `even`, `evenHead`) 
regardless of the input size, satisfying the strict in-place 
memory requirement.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* oddEvenList(ListNode* head) {
        // Handle edge cases: empty list or single node
        if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) {
            return head;
        }

        // Pointer to track the current odd-indexed node
        ListNode* odd = head;
        
        // Pointer to track the current even-indexed node
        ListNode* even = head->next;
        
        // Store the head of the even list to connect it later
        ListNode* evenHead = even;

        // Traverse until we reach the end of the list
        while (even != nullptr && even->next != nullptr) {
            // Link the current odd node to the next odd node
            odd->next = even->next;
            // Advance the odd pointer
            odd = odd->next;

            // Link the current even node to the next even node
            even->next = odd->next;
            // Advance the even pointer
            even = even->next;
        }

        // Connect the end of the odd list to the head of the even list
        odd->next = evenHead;
        
        // Return the modified original head
        return head;
    }
};
